    {
      "id": "41d7338d-9648-4f26-910a-1563aad7dabb",
      "phaseId": "5464ff74-fa17-4412-abaa-95aa19810b33",
      "number": 416,
      "shortId": "HEQ9R",
      "priority": 5,
      "shortName": "surface-scoped-keybindings-the",
      "title": "Surface-scoped keybindings: the active context owns the keyboard, and hints/menus follow it",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "**Read `docs/surface-compositor.md` §2, §3, §5 and §6 first** — recovered from git 2026-08-12 after being deleted in `3d71092` under a commit message claiming its content had been transferred. This task is that design, applied to input.\n\n# The defect\n\n**While the exposé is open, the app underneath is still being driven.**\n\n- `prefix+j` / `prefix+k` change the focused pane in the session behind the map.\n- `prefix+/` lights **KeyHints on the sidebars and the panes underneath the exposé** (Antonio,\n  2026-08-12). The letters belong to surfaces that are not the active context.\n- `prefix+p`, `prefix+e` and the rest of the app's bindings all run.\n\nAntonio: *\"In the overlay we should not be able to do a lot of prefix+key things… Each overlay\nshould declare its own keybinding and actions.\"*\n\n# Why it happens — one flag answering two questions\n\n`docs/surface-compositor.md` §2 keeps **two orthogonal mechanisms** apart:\n\n1. **Coarse — context activation.** `current_index` points at the active context surface. Opening a\n   modal sets it: *\"it's exclusive → everything beneath goes dormant.\"*\n2. **Fine — geometric occlusion.** Within the eligible set, a target is hidden iff a higher-z\n   surface's occluder covers its centre.\n\n`domain_for` (`heca/src/app/interaction.rs:212`) asks the **fine** question to decide the **coarse**\none: it calls `chrome::content_covered(state)` — a fact about what is painted over — to decide\n`Domain::Overlay`. The exposé declares `covers_content: false` because that is geometrically true\n(you can see the panes, and the hint resolver needs that), and by doing so it also, accidentally,\nsays \"the base context is still live\". One flag, two questions, and the exposé needs opposite\nanswers to them.\n\n§6's invariant names it: *\"If you find yourself special-casing a surface's hints, the surface is\nmis-modelled — fix the model, don't add a filter.\"*\n\n# What to build\n\n## 1. The coarse gate is \"is a context surface active\", not \"does something cover the content\"\n\n`domain_for` returns `Domain::Overlay` while a **modal** layer is active\n(`LayerRegistry::top_modal_index`, which exists and already orders by band). Coverage goes back to\nmeaning only what it says. The exposé keeps `covers_content: false` and still makes the base context\ndormant.\n\n## 2. What survives is `ActionPolicy::Global` — the existing classification\n\n`Domain::Overlay` already permits only `Global`. Two actions must be **reclassified**, because they\nare surface-agnostic by nature and act on whichever context is live:\n\n- `open_context_menu` (`prefix+>`)\n- the leader/hint picker (`prefix+/`)\n\n`reload_config` and `close_overlay` already are `Global`. `focus_down`, `command_palette`,\n`focus_dock` and the rest stay as they are and are therefore refused while a context is up — which\nis the wanted behaviour. **`prefix+e` is not global**: it is the workspaces component's own\n`global_focus` (`keybindings.default.toml:681`), and it should not work from inside the map.\n\n⚠️ **The map's own intents must keep working**: `close_pane_by_id`, `delete_column`,\n`delete_workspace`, `FocusPaneThenAction` and `CloseOverlay` are dispatched by the exposé's own tree\nwith `InteractionSource::Keyboard`. If the domain rule refuses them the deletes break (they were only\njust made to work — T327 item 4). Either give a layer's own tree its own `InteractionSource`, **or**\njudge by the surface the intent came from. Antonio's constraint: *\"it should be valid for all\noverlays… but each overlay might use its own actions and keybindings so we risk blocking future\nactions.\"* So it cannot be a fixed allow-list — it has to be the action's own declared policy plus\nwhere the intent came from.\n\n## 3. `prefix+/` collects only the active context's targets\n\n`chrome::active_peek_targets` walks the chrome tree, every `state.pane_headers` tree and **every\nvisible layer**. Per §3 the eligible set is *\"`current_index` plus its visible, non-blocking\ndescendants\"* — everything below the active context is suppressed. That filter is missing, which is\nwhy the letters appear on the sidebar underneath the map. Same rule for the context menu.\n\n## 4. Surface-scoped keys: `[[keys.surface]]`\n\nA dock and an overlay are the same thing to the keyboard. `[[keys.component]]`\n(`keybindings.default.toml:676`) already carries the two tiers — `global_focus = \"prefix+e\"` reaches\nthe workspaces dock from outside; `cursor_up` / `activate_selected` / `create_workspace` apply only\nwhile it holds the keyboard. Generalise the table to **`[[keys.surface]]`** with `name` = the surface\nid (`workspaces`, `expose`, a plugin's), keeping `[[keys.component]]` as an accepted spelling so\nexisting configs keep working. Shape Antonio sketched:\n\n```toml\n[[keys.surface]]\nname         = \"expose\"\nglobal_focus = \"prefix+Tab\"\ndismiss      = \"q,Escape\"\ndelete_pane  = \"x\"\n```\n\nResolution: the surface holding the keyboard first (shadowing, the way a neovim floating window's\nbuffer-local mappings shadow the global map), then the app's, then filtered by the action's policy.\n\nThis also removes today's oddity that the exposé's `x` / `r` / `d` are **literals in Rust**\n(`heca/src/chrome/expose.rs::delete_keys`) rather than bindings a user can rebind.\n\n## 5. A plugin needs nothing host-private\n\n`ActionMeta.policy` already exists (`heca/src/actions.rs:2007`) beside `args`, `confirm` and `icon`,\nand `register_dynamic` is how a name-keyed action is registered. So a plugin declares its actions'\npolicies at registration and its keys as its own surface layer. **Verify this end to end** — it is\nthe Rule Zero test for this task.\n\n# Verification (Antonio drives)\n\nOpen the exposé: `prefix+j/k` do nothing · `prefix+/` lights letters **only on the map's cards** ·\n`prefix+>` opens the map's own menu · `prefix+Shift+r` still reloads · `ctrl+j/k` still move the\nmap's cursor · `x`/`r`/`d` still delete. Then the same with a dock focused, to confirm nothing\nregressed there.",
      "notes": "",
